1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Bo Stensson, till Bergunda (Natt och Dag)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Sten Bosson (Natt och Dag) and Ingeborg Karlsdotter, Vinäsätten 
2 CONT * Bo Stensson Natt och Dag,  Night and Day Noble (No. 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Natt_och_Dag_nr_13#TAB_12 Adelsvapen, Night and Day No. 13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bo Stenssonto Bergunda in Bergunda sn / G, † before 1477. Named knight (RHKn.) 1408-10-23. Participated in rafting in Östergötland 1413 (RHKn.). District chief in Bankekind's district (RHKn.) 1414, 1435 and still 1464. Riksråd 143
2 CONC 0-1460. Joined the uprising against King Erik in 1434 and in 1436, together with his brother Bengt, was given command of Kalmar, which Karl Knutsson in 1439 deprived them of. He then went to King Erik, but entered into a settlemen
2 CONC t with Karl Knutsson in 1440. Sealed as k. Was King Charles' council 1453 and 1455, (RHKn.) But participated in the meeting in Skara 1458-01-21 in the election of the eldest son of King Kristiern Vasa as his successor (RHKn.). He 
2 CONC lived at least from 1431 on Bergunda, then on Eksjö Hovgård in Vallsjö parish, Jönköping county, '''Probably married long before 1426-04-24 to Katarina Svensdotter Sture''' who made her will 1464-07-12, and was dead 1469-08-20, wh
2 CONC en her husband gave land to Nydala monastery for her and for the sons Mr. Svante Bosson and Sven Stures souls. Daughter of the knight and councilor Sven Nilsson Sture d.ä. and Gunnild Matsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Svante Bosson''' He or possibly his brother Sven (Sb II. P. 367.) entered the service of the Prussian Grand Master Paul von Rusdorf as a young man in 1435. Knight in Sweden 1450. Lived 1460. Died unmarried probably long befor
2 CONC e 1469.
2 CONT * '''Sven Bosson Sture,''' dead young. Mentioned as deceased 1469-08-20.
2 CONT * '''Nils Bosson Sture,''' Died 1494. Knight and Councilor. Ancestor of the Stureätten of Natt and Dagsläkten or the so-called Young Stureätten . See Tab. 75
2 CONT * '''Märta Bosdotter,''' married to Peder Ribbing .
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/mobil/Artikel/8774 Bo Stensson (Night and Day) District Chief, Knight, Riksråd]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Swedish Biographical Dictionary'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Bo Stensson, brother of N 4 , d between 1469 and 1477 (Smål arch). The earliest mentioned 08 o was then a knight, häradsh in Bankekind, Ög, at least 14–64, councilor at least from 30 o still 60, holder of Kalmar castle county to
2 CONC gether with brother N 4 late summer 36 – Oct 39, got Sundholms län (probably Västra hd) o Södra Vedbo hd in Småland and two parishes in Östergötland as a grant Oct 39, possibly once again holder of Kalmar castle 44 or 45. 
2 CONT
2 CONT G no later than 1424 (probably much earlier with regard to a son's age) m Katarina Svensdtr, d between 1464 ( Lund Archdiocese's record book) o 1469, dtr to the knight Sven Sture o Gunnild Matsdtr.
2 CONT
2 CONT B was already a knight when he first appears in 1408 in now known source material, which is why his knighthood dubbing may have taken place at Queen Philip's coronation in Lund in the autumn of 1406 (cf. vol. 16, p. 25). The evide
2 CONC nce of 1408 is in King Erik and Queen Margaret's receipt for his father's then recently deceased servant Sten Bengtsson's (Bielke; vol. 4) heirs on his pawn. In addition to B, his father Sten Bosson (N 2), his uncle Knut Bosson (N
2 CONC  3) and his older brother Bengt Stensson also appear among this receipt's.(N 4). In 1411, like N 4 and their uncle Nils Bosson, he was among the numerous Nordic nobles who acted as pledges for King Erik in a letter dated in Koldin
2 CONC g, Jutland, about the truce with Holstein. In the autumn of 1413, B was the foremost of the secular assistants at the raffle meetings held in the diocese of Linköping under the presidium of his uncle N 3 and the Östgötland lawyer.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT No later than 1414, B succeeded the bailiff Esbjörn Djäkn(vol. 14) as district chief in Bankekind in Östergötland, although at least 1431 he lived in Bergunda in Bergunda sn in Värend; his father's manor Ekhult, however, should be
2 CONC  identical with Ekhult in Björsäter's sn in Bankekind. He would hold this office for at least half a century, although he mostly let deputies handle it. After him it would be held by his descendants for a couple of generations. B 
2 CONC can not be appointed as councilor until 1430. Like his uncle N 3 , he was one of the councilors who, together with other saviors under pressure from Engelbrekt Engelbrektsson 16 Aug 1434 in Vadstena, by letter declared King Erik f
2 CONC aith and obedience. Together with this uncle and his brother N 4B also appears among the twelve issuers of the manifesto dated in Sthlm 12 Sept 1434 to Norway, the Grand Master of Prussia, the Livonian Order country and the Wendis
2 CONC h Hanseatic cities about the reasons for the Swedish uprising. These manifestos were never sent, but on 3 Oct N 3 and B together with their cousin Nils Eringislason (Hammerstaätten; vol. 18) from Söderköping wrote to the Grand Mas
2 CONC ter and asked for help against King Erik, with a copy of the manifesto of 12 Sept attached. According to the Karlskrönikan, he participated in the failed siege of Stegeholm in northeastern Småland together with, among others, the 
2 CONC future king Karl Knutsson (Farmer) and Nils Eringislason's son Eringisl Nilsson(vol. 18). In the preliminary conciliation treaty between King Erik and the Swedes entered into in Halmstad in the spring of 1435, B is named as well a
2 CONC s Karl Knutsson among the witnesses, and together with his brother N 4 he also appears among the 18 people who represented the Swedes in the final conciliation treaty with Erik in Sthlm this autumn. During the renewed uprising in 
2 CONC the spring of 1436, according to the Charles Chronicle, he first became one of the two whom Engelbrekt appointed to lead the siege of Laholm and later one of the commanders in Varberg (cf. vol. 3, p. 774). 
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Erik at the settlement in Kalmar in the late summer of 1436 was forced to agree that Sthlm, Kalmar and Nyköping's castle counties would also be administered by Swedes, he handed over Kalmar to B and his brother N 4 accor
2 CONC ding to the Karlskrönikan. In the late autumn of 1437, B's wife mortgaged two farms in Halland, whereupon she stated that she had run into financial difficulties because her husband was not in the country but a city in his "lordsh
2 CONC ip" affairs (Nilsson). In a later deleted section of the chronicle it is stated that the drots Kristiern Nilsson (Vaasa) and Karl Knutsson, now marsh, in 1438 challenged the two brothers Kalmar; they then declared themselves unabl
2 CONC e to leave it to anyone other than the king, after which the drotsen and the marsh began a siege, which, however, ended in conciliation. In connection with the conflict between Karl Knutsson and B's brother Nils Stensson(N 6) at t
2 CONC he beginning of 1439, according to the chronicle, B must have followed this brother to King Erik on Gotland, and this information is confirmed by Erik's proclamations on 19 March. These also state that Karl Knutsson captured and l
2 CONC ooted B's and brother N 4 's wives and children. Following a standstill agreement in Arkösund on the Östgötland coast 21 Aug, B together with N 4 and N 6at the beginning of October, according to the Karlskrönikan, appeared at a me
2 CONC eting in (Söder) Tälje and handed over Kalmar to Karl Knutsson. B would instead get Sundholms län (probably Västra hd in Småland), which was held by at least 1416-23 by his probably father-in-law who died in 1423 or 1424, as well 
2 CONC as half Vedbo hd in Småland and two parishes in Östergötland, a grant that partially (Västra hd and Södra Vedbo) came to own stocks for several generations (Westin; cf. Schück 1959, p. 329). Only after further conflict and N 6's d
2 CONC eath did B and N 4 enter into a final settlement with Karl Knutsson on a lord's day in Arboga 13 Jan 1440. In the settlement between him and Kristiern Nilsson in 1441, however, they appear among the pledges for the latter together
2 CONC  with, among others, his son-in-law Bengt Jönsson (Oxenstierna).
2 CONT
2 CONT An undated letter from 1444 or 1445 states that after the death of the then castle chief Gustaf Sture, King Kristofer wanted to leave Kalmar for a "Mr. Bo", who was identified with B. However, there is no evidence that he really o
2 CONC nce again came into possession of Kalmar ( Kumlien 1933). During Kristofer's reign, B participated in council meetings in Khvn 1442, 1445 and 1447 and in Kalmar 1443 and 1444 (Olesen), and he also participated in his expedition to
2 CONC  Gotland in 1446. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In connection with the royal election in 1448, B is found as well as his brother N 4 and his son N 7among Karl Knutsson's opponents. During his reign he appears in the treaties with Denmark in Sthlm 1453 on stagnation and in Vadst
2 CONC ena 1455 on peace. After Kristian I's ascension to the throne, B 1458 participated as the oldest member of the Riksdag in the election of his eldest son as heir to the throne, in the confirmation of King Kristofer's morning gift t
2 CONC o Dorotea remarried to Christian I, in the judgment on confiscation of Karl Knutsson's estates and in confirmation of Hanseatic city privileges. . After 1460 he can not be proved to have participated in council meetings. Even befo
2 CONC re the change of throne in 1457, B's last surviving son Nils Sture had married a cousin of Karl Knutsson, and before his death, the former generally Karl-Knutsson-hostile B experienced that this son became one of Karl's main suppo
2 CONC rters. Through Nils Sture, he became the grandfather of the head of stateSvante Nilsson and grandfather's father to the head of state Sten Sture dy .
2 CONT
