2 CONT B. came home just right to participate in the association that Council members entered in Skara in the summer of 1322 to end the duchess Ingeborg's and Knut Porse's power and instead take the guardian board in their own hands. As 
2 CONC the guardian government's representative, B. the following year, when it is ordered, all county owners in Uppland school to B. pay the third of the income they had to collect, to be used for the nation's needs. In the following ye
2 CONC ars his name also appears in some records. We do not know B's year of birth, but judging by the time he as a national councilor and the lawman participated in public life, he should have come quite a few years ago when returning f
2 CONC rom the pilgrimage. October 21st In 1326 he writes at Finstad his will. Primarily among the stately row of scholarships for church foundations, a gift is given to S: t Nikolai chapel in Uppsala Cathedral, where B. and his other wi
2 CONC fe chose their camp town, now excellent by a splendid soapstone stone. Then follow the gifts to Uppsala Cathedral (for instituting a prebende), to all the cathedrals in the kingdom and a lot of monasteries and other foundations. I
2 CONC n 1327 died B. One of our most outstanding pieces of medieval history gives us a specified task on the expenses of his funeral (for all kinds of fabrics, wine, French and rhino, a variety of spices, etc.), ending at a total of 234
2 CONC  mark money, to which came, among other things. a. 30 mark for fairs and alms. - There was only a small part of B's ​​fortune, which went to the legacy. B. was certainly one of Sweden's richest men of his time. We still have the l
2 CONC ist of the properties which, after his death, were divided between his son Israel and his sons Ulv and Magnus Gudmars sons,
2 CONT
2 CONT The comparatively abundant but rather impersonal material we have for a biography of B. admits only to take his picture with rough and little individualized traits: a practical ability over the ordinary measure, shown in public as
2 CONC  in individual recruitments, not least in the management of their own finances, and as a complement a highly prominent religiosity, completely bearing the correct church character and taking shape in ancient forms. Longer than to 
2 CONC these qualities, common to so many medieval great men, one can probably not come. But perhaps in our medieval history we hardly have anyone who unites them to a greater extent than B. - https://www.geni.com/photo/view/600000000260
