1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Ramborg Israelsdotter (Finsta)==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Israel Birgersson (Finsta) and Bengta Carlsdotter Färla 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Married==
2 CONT
2 CONT Israel Birgersson, teammate Birger's son, had three children, of which Ramborg and Peter certainly, as well as Helena probably in Mr. Israel's famous marriage. Of these, Ramborg became Israelsdotter g 1) m Filip Nilsson (troll Spa
2 CONC rre of Tofta) and 2) m knight, the Swedish National Council and the lawman Anund Jonsson (Lion's face) and Helena Israelsdotter with the knight, the national council, the marsh and the lawman Karl Ulfsson (Sparre of Tofta).  - htt
2 CONC ps://sok.riksarkivet.se/Sbl/Mobil/Artikel/14142
2 CONT
2 CONT *  (1) '''Filip Nilsson (Sparre av Tofta)'''
2 CONT * (2) '''Anund Jonsson (lion face), knight, national council and lawman.''' Anund married 1st time with Ramborg Israelsdotter, Finsta + Approx 1383
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children with Anund Jonsson (lion face) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Christina Anundsdotter''' ~ Lion's face, † 21/10 1440-10/5 1441, Buried Vadstena monastery 7/2 139, married Anund A Sture to Grönskog.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Handwritten article about Öråker by Herman Svenngård.==
2 CONT
2 CONT The oldest task we have about Öråker is in Vadstena monastery land register. Among other things, it says that Tuna was donated to Vadstena monastery, along with Asker and Öråker on the 10th of December 1384 by knight Anund Jonsson
2 CONC  (lion's face). Anund Jonsson's deceased wife,Ramborg Israelsdotter (two turned wings) had received the farms in the morning gift from her first husband Philip Nilsson, and uttered a wish that they would be donated to the monaster
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vadstena monastery land register for the year 1447 also states that Öråker at that time was a settlerp to Tuna ie the tower was within the Bolby village of Tuna's land, but there constituted a separate farm with a fence yard. The 
2 CONC lease to the monastery was 4 pounds of grain and 1 land in money and it was used by a countryman, ie a tenant under the monastery. In 1466, Landbon was called Pedher and he had the same lease as landbon in 1447. Data is missing un
2 CONC til 1529, but that year, men's meeting was held in Strängnäs on 17-21 June. One councilor from Bro from here participated, namely Jon in Asker. This men's meeting confirmed Västerås recess.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1540 and 1550 was called the farmer Jöns Finn. In 1552, the domicile is called 1/2 settlement and consisted of 6 ores. The user was called Bertil. It looks as if two countrymen have arrived at home, namely Bengt and Lasse,
2 CONC  and were now 2 ears country larger. From 1542-1680, Öråker was the tax home and we know very little about the fate of the farm. There is some information about the names of the residents. From 1597-1622 he was named Thomas. In 16
2 CONC 10, he and his wife were charged a new tax, namely, a team of six pawns for themselves and three ground for their two children and one girl. This tax was soon baked into other taxes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1624, a new name appears in the documents, namely Öråkerstorpet. There, 1624 and a few years ahead live wife Malin, probably the widow of some country resident. In 1666, the turf is declared a fate.
2 CONT 1   Ålsta1.doc   2011-11-21     96-04-11
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The medieval Upplands-Bro, from the Medieval Sweden==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1: 7 Attundaland Bro hurray Published by the National Heritage Board in 1992, page 25'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1274 the Ask ... in a mansione Ask (DS 582), 1289 the Ask (DS 1007), 1309 the Asker (DS 1614) - 10 I, 9 b. UH 2, vial, 1546 2, 0: 5 + 0: 5, 1566 1 kl, 1: 2. (Körsviks rd) Sum jt 1: 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asker is probably a courtyard for a sibling group, which is due to A in the late 13th and early 14th century. B Matsson establishes 1274 testaments for himself and his wife Lucia on the 'Sätesgården A' (mansio) (DS 582). In 1289, 
2 CONC Agnes Matsdotter establishes in her her will and gives money to the church and the vicar in Näs (DS 1007). In 1298 Rörik Matsson is mentioned in A (DS 1233); the same year writes, also to Tuna, this sn (DS 1215). Rörik is mentione
2 CONC d in 1287-96 as 'R by Sollentuna' (DS 949, 1744), which is related to the fact that he is the holder of the eleventh canonical at Uppsala Cathedral, to which Sollentuna church is the grant (DS 3850).
2 CONT Agnes son Jacob in A (ox head in broken shield) will bequeathing money to the church and the vicar in Näs, when his sons Harald and Birger were buried; they will now be moved to Sigtuna (DS 1614). In 1312, Jacob is mentioned in A 
2 CONC widow Ingrid (DS 1856)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1384, Anund Jonsson (Lion's Face) surrenders his wife Ramborg lsraelsdotter's (Finsta-eden) estate in A to Vadstena monastery (RAP 10 / I 2; see Tuna).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1397, Karl Ulfsson (Sparre of Tofta) certifies that Rambor Israelsdotter (Finsta-ätten) against renouncing his part in the solver, year plant and equipment on her husband Filip Nilsson's (Sparre of Tofta) breaking farms received 0
2 CONC :10 in A we Öråker in Näs sn, where Ramborg's morning gift lay in Tuna and Öråker (RAp 10/2, SRP 2845).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1447-1502 Vadstena monastery has two farms together 0: 9: 2. They each interest 4 pd grain and 1: 0 pn. In 1466, landbon settles on one farm 2 pounds of grain for 2: 0 pn, while the other remains with 2 pounds, which later pays. I
2 CONC n 1447, the interest rate was previously reported to have been 10 pounds of grain and 5: 0 for the two farms together. It is further stated that the two farms are settlers under Tuna. (VaKIJb)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1529 Jon, a farmer in A 'on behalf of the country' participates at the parliament in Strängnäs (GR 6 p. 178). 1538/41 has Vadstena monastery two farms at 0: 5 each of which pays 4 pounds of grain, 0: 5 avradspn, 1⁄2: 0 reciprocati
2 CONC ng point and 4 horses, 1541 4 pounds of grain, 1: 0 pn, 4 bishops and 2 king horses ( UH 1538: 2). In 1559, the two heritage and the oak farms each have seed for 3 pounds, meadow to 25 lass, good forest, good mulching and fishing 
2 CONC waters (AoE 29 A). Aspvik, 1302 Aspuic (Skoklb p. 17), 1303 in Aspwiik (DS 13, 10 I, 9 b. UH 3 shoe, 1566 cl, 1: 0 + 1: 0 + 1: 0. 1 shovel (some years shook). Total 3: 0. The village is 1566 - 68 granted Måns Persson printer 24 - 
2 CONC http://www.ukforsk.se/dms_mantal/DMS_bro_nas.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT ==SETTINGS FROM THE CULTURAL GEOGRAPHICAL INSTITUTION June 2015, A lost Tuna==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A study of the village of Tuna on the Lennartsnäh peninsula, by Olle Södergren'''
2 CONT '''Kloster donation 1384'''
2 CONT
2 CONT A couple of generations later, we find a letter that gives us a clearer picture of the ownership conditions on the Lennartsnäshalvön during the 14th century. It can be seen here that the dragon Asbjörn Sixtensson (Sparre of Tofta)
2 CONC  and his heirs had gathered all the eastern part of the peninsula. In this gift letter, knight Anund Jonsson (Lion's Face) clarified that he fulfilled his deceased wife Ramborg Israel's daughter's (by Finstaätten) desire to donate
2 CONC  the assets she received in the morning gift in her previous marriage to Vadstena monastery. This transaction included Tuna, Öråker and Asker, which probably means the entire eastern part of the peninsula. However, there is no ind
2 CONC ication of whether other settlements existed in the southeastern parts of the peninsula. Ramborg's former husband Filip Nilsson (Sparre of Tofta) was the son of Nils Abjörsson, and grandchild of the murderer Asbjörn Sixtensson (Sp
2 CONC arre of Tofta). The family had thereby acquired significant assets in the area during the 14th century. The properties were inherited by Filip Nilsson's wife Ramborg after his passing, and then on to her new husband Anund who comp
2 CONC leted her wish for the monastery donation. (SDHK-no: 12757) Here I want to link back to the original acquisition of the farm in Tuna 1307, where the dukes Erik and Valdemar donated the goods to their dragon Asbjörn Sixtensson (Spa
2 CONC rre of Tofta). This transaction took place in the middle of the civil war called the Håtunaleken, where Erik, supported by Valdemar, sought to seize power from his younger brother King Birger Magnusson, and shortly thereafter he b
2 CONC eheaded his guardian Torgils Knutsson. The dukes were allies with Norway while King Birger was allied with Denmark. In 1307, King Birger was imprisoned while the Duke brothers in January managed to negotiate a truce that lasted un
2 CONC til December of the same year (Wadbring 2008). My interpretation is that the donation of Tuna to Asbjörn Sixtensson touched on the crown or the imprisoned king's property, which the duke brothers during the ceasefire suited to han
2 CONC d out to their allies, as a reward for their support. In another letter it appears that the dukes had donated another property in the parish a month earlier, namely Aspvik's mill a few kilometers from Tuna, which the king had used
2 CONC  for his own use (DS 1548) according to the gift letter. I mean that it is possible and likely that the circumstances of Sixtensson's acquisition of Tuna are something that has remained in the memory of men and beyond. Possibly th
2 CONC is has stigmatized the possession of the property and contributed to Ramborg Israel's daughter's desire to donate the property to the monastery Big eggs and possible medieval economy The ownership of the peninsula during the 13th 
2 CONC century is unclear and I have found few clues that can say anything about the agriculture organization, but I believe that there can nevertheless be a basis for an interpretation. The supposed family group consisting of B. Matsson
2 CONC , Rörik Mattsson, Agnes Matsdotter and Jacob Agnesson owned properties both in Asker and Tuna at the end of the 13th century. Ashtrays are described with the term "mansione", while Asbjörn's gift items in Tuna are described with "
2 CONC curiam", both of which could indicate a large-scale farm operation with a subordinate home. However, this does not necessarily have to be the case. It can nevertheless be the name of a larger farm, without saying anything about th
2 CONC e form of organization (see Hansson 2001 p.39). I want to make the interpretation with caution that Asker is the basis for the family group with the father Mats, who then put some of the homeowners in Tuna to their farm under the 
2 CONC main farm in Asker. Based on my account of the background for Asbjörn's donated farm, I mean that it probably did not belong to the family from Asker, but was a crown goods at the time of the donation. Rörik Mattsson signed up to 
2 CONC Tuna 1298 and the family from Asker thus also had a physical presence in the village, even when the time for the acquisition of this property is unclear. If the presumed crown goods in Tuna had conducted large-scale farming at the
2 CONC  time of the acquisition, I mean that it would seem unlikely that one would have cut off parts of the goods for the benefit of an adjacent manor. I mean that this could be interpreted as an indication of farm operations in Tuna at
2 CONC  the time of the Asker-based family acquisition. However, this does not preclude the fact that there has previously been a centralized operation of agriculture in the village under a main farm. The place name Tuna, like the name "
2 CONC curiam", can be said to support such an interpretation. Previous research has shown that it is possible to see a change in the organization of agriculture during the 13th century, where former large farms in some cases completely 
2 CONC switched to rural farming and gave rise to the formation of villages. Villages that are later under single owners are said to be an indicator of previous economies of scale. (Berg 2003 p.94). At the end of the 14th century, Asbjör
2 CONC n Sixtensson's descendants owned the entire eastern peninsula. Whether or not the acquisition of Asker and other parts of these coherent major properties took place shortly after his entry into the village, or if it was an extende
2 CONC d process is unclear. On the other hand, this can be assumed to have had significant consequences for the organization of farm operations. I understand that this change of ownership represents a shift between what can be called a 
2 CONC low-resident to a high-resident. This is a term that has been coined in the past for various status layers within the salvation, where the low-salvation had a tangible local anchorage and presence in the village, and no alternativ
2 CONC e main yard. The High Redeemer, on the other hand, had many other possessions, and thus did not have the same presence or anchorage in the local countryside, nor his identity attached to the property in the same way. (Hansson 2001
2 CONC  p.46) I mean that the family from Asker seems to have been based in the village, and thus probably drove agriculture on their own under the family farm in Asker. Furthermore, I mean that it is likely that Asbjörn Sixtensson with 
2 CONC descendants had a much more modest presence, and relied on passive taxation of independent countrymen. Asbjörn Sixtensson's original donation from the duke brothers shows that in addition to the farm in Tuna he also received a num
2 CONC ber of other properties, including a manor in Vikbolandet and all the mills in Torshälla.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gift of the great possessions on the Lennartsnal peninsula to Vadstena monastery in 1384 marks the start of a long period of ecclesiastical ownership of the eastern part of the peninsula, which contributed to keeping the prope
2 CONC rty together. (DS 1554) - https://www.academia.edu/13042303/Ett_försvunnet_Tuna._En_studie_av_byn_Tuna_på_Lennartsnäshalvön
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History & Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anund Jonsson Lejonansikte to Ringstaholm and Almare-Ståk was the knight, the National Council and the Lagman on Öland. He signed 1396 Nykøbing Recessen on The choice of Eric of Pomerania as King. With Ramborg Finsta, he got 1 d
2 CONC aughter Kristina, Married to the National Council of Anund Algotsson Sture - http://www.gravstenogepitafier.dk/vadstena3.htm
2 CONT * Peter Israelsson, with the consent of his son, replaces Anund Jonsson and sister Ramborg Israelsdotter to his cousin ("sẏslunga") knight Birger Ulfsson his entire property in Klället (in Döderhult's parish), which he inherited f
2 CONC rom his father, against the whole of Birger Ulfsson's property in Sörvik , Djupeträsk, Manketorp, Södra Saltvik (now Saltvik) and Djurhult (all in Döderhult's parish) in accordance with what is said in the letter Birger Ulfsson wr
2 CONC ote to (the benefit of) the issuer.  The issuer, together with Bo Jonsson, seals in Sweden, the issuer's gull Anund Jonsson, knight, and sister Mrs Ramborg Israelsdotter. Orig. on the perg. (25.4 x 16.2 cm, fold up 3.5 cm; 15 rows
2 CONC ), Sv. National Archives. Printed: E. Grönblad, New sources of Finland's medieval history I (1857), p. 13 (no. 6).  Regest: FMU nr 852. About Peter Israelsson (Finstaätten), his sister Ramborg Israelsdotter and the gull knight Anu
2 CONC nd Jonsson (lion face) see ASF I, p. 36 f. About Peter Israelsson's cousin Birger Ulfsson (Ulvåsaätten), see a.a. p. 95.  About the locations in Döderhults parish see DMS, Småland 4: 2, p. 157 ff.  Compare letter 1376 16/11 (DS no
2 CONC . 9363) in which Birger Ulfsson receives land in Klället from knight Erik Karlsson and his wife Märta Knutsdotter. - http://www3.ra.se/sdhk/pdf/10912.pdf
2 CONT * From Vadstena monastery land register .(Historical documents part 16). Handwritten note 1384-12-10 Tuna along with Asker and Öråker was donated 10/11 1384 by knight Anund Jonsson (lion face) whose deceased wife Ramborg Israelsdo
2 CONC tter (two turned wings) received the farms in the morning gift of her 1st husband Philip Nilsson. She had wished that they had to be given to Vadstena Monastery. The daughter Kristina Anundsdotter and her husband knight Anund Stur
2 CONC e confirmed 3/4 1425. - http://www.bygdeband.se/wp-content/uploads/uploaded/385/287384_raker1.doc	
2 CONT
