2 CONC ainst "Humblagaardhin" in Härene in Kålland district.''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original:  Or. perg. RA 0101  images, Post-medieval copies:  Brocman: IV Misc. 45:15, Literature and commentary, Cf. SDHK no. 7706 (1360) and 19678 (1421).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN LAKE, KNIGHT ERIK BJÖRNSON in BOHUSLÄN, TRE ROSER and BISHOP BRYNOLF GERLACKSSON.=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Nils Fredrik Beerståhl 1997.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Annestorp-Anstenstorp in Lägnum and its medieval owners.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anstenstorp in Lägnum parish, now Annestorp, is a salvation home, whose fate can be traced back 600 years. As a rule, you cannot find out anything about the owners of ordinary farms that did not have a manor character before the m
2 CONC iddle of the 16th century, when the land registers were published. But in the matter of Annestorp, the estate is covered in four medieval letters: 1397 1463 1479 and 1489. The letter 1397 is in the National Archives Copenhagen and
2 CONC  gives the names of two owners at the time. Albrecht of Mecklenburg had recently been deposed as king in Sweden and Queen Margareta of Denmark-Norway had been summoned by the Swedish nobles. It soon turned out, however, that she w
2 CONC as a woman with hard nips_ a Margaret Thatcher of the time on the Swedish throne. Just during the 1390s, she carried out a reduction that affected those who called her in. One of these Nils Jönsson Svarte Skåning. had been the but
2 CONC ler at Axvalla house, thus in practice the governor of Skaraborg.
2 CONT
2 CONT A couple of the letters in Copenhagen that concern him are lists of farms that partly he himself, partly the squire Harald Lake at Malma, transcribes to Queen Margareta. But Nils Jönsson Svarte Skåning has apparently found out abo
2 CONC ut the queen's reduction plans at an early stage and managed to save a good part of his fortune. This is what another letter is about. A long line of properties in Västergötland are enumerated with careful notes for each one that 
2 CONC they were returned by the seller himself or a relative of his. Svarte Skåning has received back the purchase price and can breathe a sigh of relief with the coffers well-filled with ready money. One of these reclaimed farms is Ans
2 CONC tenstorp in Längum in Viste district and the one who now (1397) owns the property is Toke or Tyke Jonsson. (note 1). Peculiarly enough, it is a member of the Lake family, who is mentioned in connection with Anstenstorp in the next
2 CONC  letter of 1463. (note 2). There has been succession after a savior Torbjörn Slätt and a series of savior homes have been divided between the seagulls Bertil Perdersson (Lilliehöök) to Kolbäck and Karl Lake to Malma. Anstenstorp h
2 CONC as fallen to Lake's lot. Apart from the widow, he left behind the second wife; Katarina Benktsdotter, son Harald Karlsson, who did not use the name Lake. Actually, then Anstenstorp should have continued in the Lake family, but tha
2 CONC t did not happen. In 1479, the farm was donated to Skara Cathedral by Norwegian knight Erik Björnsson from Bro parish in Bohuslän. In somewhat modernized language, the letter reads: (note 3). "I, Erik Björnsson at Radaberg, solemn
2 CONC ly agree to have given my dear wife Lucia Pedersdotter's consent to a fraternity in Skara Cathedral which the venerable Father God Bishop Brynolf founded there, a farm in Astenstorp in Längum's parish in Viste härad - for the blis
2 CONC s of our souls and our dear children Olof Eriksson and Mette Eriksdotter, who are deceased and Sidsel Eriksdotter, who is still alive.- In addition, of course, I ask for the seal (seal) of Reverend Father Bishop Brynolfs and virtu
2 CONC ous man Jöns Knutsson (note 4) under this letter with my own. Written and registered at Radaberg." Erik Björnsson på Röe, as the manor house is now called, called hövitsman på Bohus in 1448, was recently knighted in 1450, when Chr
2 CONC istian I was crowned in Trondheim and still appears in 1483 at the coronation of King Hans. He was a Norwegian councilor and carried the same coat of arms with a six-part shield as the scarab bishop Brynolf Gerlaksson. (note 5) Sp
2 CONC eaking is the knight Erik Björnsson 1479 seals the donation letter with broken (altered) stamp; diagonally across the six-squared shield runs a broad beam from the upper left corner. Perhaps as an expression of respect for fellow-
2 CONC sigilant bishop Brynolf. In 1489 the widow Mrs. Lucia Pedersdotter (Oxe) is mentioned and in the same year the next transaction regarding Anstenstorp takes place. Harald Karlsson (Lake) at Malma could not forget the fine and well-
2 CONC located property in the district, which his father was once awarded. Now that the donor was dead, in 1489 he made a push against the dom chapter in Skara to get it back. And this despite the fact that the receiver and co-sealer, B
2 CONC ishop Brynolf, was still sitting in a highly desired state of well-being on the Skara bishop's chair. The bishop must have been a reasonable person, because he did not oppose the transaction. But it was probably against him handli
2 CONC ng the business himself, so he delegated the matter to his chief justice, Olof Pedersson. (note 6). "I, Olof Pedersson, dean of Skara, let everyone know that according to my gracious bishop Brynolf's command, I was on a legal exch
2 CONC ange between the brotherhood of Skara and the well-to-do man Harald Karlsson, so that prescribed cows bequeathed to the aforementioned Harald a farm called Anstenstorp located in Viste härad in the parish of Längum which farm was 
2 CONC given and acquired by the aforementioned Harald's parents and Harald instead left the choir a farm lying in Laske härad in Väster Bitterna parish called Hallagården" How can these three seemingly contradictory letters from 1463, 1
2 CONC 479 and 1489 together.
