1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Sigrid the Fair==
2 CONT
2 CONT * According to Margareta Klausdotter's familiar, partly purely historical story of "Sigrid the fair" of lower burden than the man.  Sigrid is known to the afterlife through the romantic late-medieval tale of the battle between Ben
2 CONC gt Lagman and Birger Earl , in which the Earl must have been disarmed by Sigrid's beauty and wisdom. In fact, Sigrid and Bengt Magnusson married long after the earl of Birger Earl. The blessing is also known through Frans Hedberg'
2 CONC s Wedding at Ulvåsa from 1865.
2 CONT * Sigrid died in 1289 and buried in Skänninge Dominican Monastery.  was married to the lawyer in Östergötland , Bengt Magnusson.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Bengt Magnusson,''' son of Magnus Bengtsson. Was the layman in Östergötland, mentioned as such for the first time in 1269. Mentioned as a member of the Swedish National Council on September 11, 1288. Must have taken 
2 CONC off on pilgrimage in 1289. Again active as a layman in 1291-1292. It is said to have died in 1294, which, however, seems to be in conflict with the fact that his son Magnus is mentioned as lawman already in 1293. Was first married
2 CONC  to Margareta , possibly born from Tjust, who was dead in 1275, when the husband of, among others. a. The salvation of her soul donated property in Östergötland and in Tjust hd in Småland to a canonate in Linköping, and then was m
2 CONC arried to Sigrid, who died in 1289 and is buried in Skänninge dominican monastery. According to Margareta Klausdotter's familiar, partly purely historical story"Sigrid the fair" of lower burden than the man.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2. '''Ingeborg Bengtsdotter''' Died in September 1314 and buried in Uppsala Cathedral. According to her will on the deathbed, a number of grants would be paid to Uppsala Cathedral and several monasteries. The inheritance after h
2 CONC er was changed in 1320. Apart from a few difficult-identified properties, it consisted of land in Aspelands, Handburds, Sevede and Stranda hd, all in Småland. Was 20.2 1301 married to the lawman in Uppland the knight and the natio
2 CONC nal council Birger Petersson ( Finstaätten ) to Finsta, who was previously married to Kristina Johansdotter, who died in 1293 and was the daughter of the knight Johan Elofsson (the family of the Elof Sons ) and his wife, a daughte
2 CONC r of Mr Karl Tjelvason ( Fånöätten). Mr. Birger died on 3.4 1327. In the marriage between him and Ingeborg Bengtsdotter was born, among other things. a. the holy 
2 CONT * '''Ramborg Bengtsdotter''' First mentioned in 1313 and then called wife. Lived another 1349. Buried in Riseberga monastery. Owned property in Grimsten's hd in Närke and Lysings hd in Östergötland. According to Margareta Klausdot
2 CONC ter, he was married to an otherwise unknown Erengisl, who apparently died long before his wife and is said to have been buried in Svanhals in Lysings hd in Östergötland.
2 CONT * '''Katarina Bengtsdotter''' Stayed another 1350 at Aspenäs in Malexander's snow in Outer hd in Östergötland. Sold, for example, with his children 's consent Rönnäs in Asby snow in the same height. Was 5.9 1319, well for a long t
2 CONC ime, married to the knights knight Knut Jonsson ( Aspenäsätt ), who is mentioned as living 1292–1345 but was dead 18.3 1347.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source'''  Folkunga family  - https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Folkunga%C3%A4tten#TAB_12
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Stories from Swedish history / Anders Fryxell==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 9,'''
2 CONT
2 CONT About Bengt the teammate and Sigrid the beautiful. (NOTE! Skröna, confusion between Birger Jarl and his brother with this later name, Bengt Magnusson. Birger Jarl had been dead about 10 years when Bengt married Sigrid the second t
2 CONC ime).
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger Earl's all brothers were famous men. His one brother, Eskil, was a lawman in Westergötland, and married a very distinguished qwinna from Norrge. His second brother, Karl, was bishop of Linköping; his sister, Magnhild, was m
2 CONC arried to Härwed Ulf or Trolle, a rather rich and powerful man *). The third brother was named Bengt, and was the teammate in Östergötland. He took very strong love for a noble maiden named Sigrid, which neither was either rich or
2 CONC  noble, but so beautiful that she was generally called Sigrid the fair. Earl Birger would not hear of this, for he thought the maiden was too little for her kindred, though she was noble; but Bengt Lawman did not heed his brother'
2 CONC s will out here, but married Sigrid, his unspoken.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Earl found this, she sent Bengt a coat, one half of which was made of precious golden cloth, but the other of coarse and thin wadmal. By this he meant the dissimilarity that existed between Bengts and Sigrid's genealogy, 
2 CONC in this way also scaring Bengt to separate Sigrid from himself. But in this place, he allowed the wadmalt to fill with gold, pearls and precious stones, so that the wadmals half remained the same, if not more expensive, than the o
2 CONC ther, then sent the cloak back to the earl instead of another swar. Of this, the Earl was even more enraged and greeted back with the commandment, that he would orally speak to his brother about this matter, adding in his ifwer so
2 CONC me severe threats, which the envoy also told Bengt.
2 CONT
2 CONT A little afterwards the Earl lets saddle his horses and rides with great company to Ulfåsa, where Bengt lived, intending to put an end to this matter. As soon as Bengt saw the earl's journey from afar, he escaped to the woods and 
2 CONC left Sigrid alone at home to meet the angry brother-in-law, after he had given her good advice, however, how she ought to behave there. So she adorned herself with her most expensive clothes and most expensive jewelry, and her bea
2 CONC uty there seemed even greater. As the Earl rode into the yard, Sigrid the fair stepped out, negated him for word, and received him with all the honor and whispers. But the earl was so moved by her beauty that he forgot his cruelty
2 CONC , ran down quickly from his haste, and took Sigrid into his arms, saying, "If my brother had this undone, I would do it myself;" whereupon he was let into the house and evaded. But Bengt was brought back straight from the woods, a
2 CONC nd was then rejoiced and reconciled between the brothers instead of the enmity enmity.
2 CONT
2 CONT But Bengt teamman and Sigrid the fair lived a long time together on Ulfåsa and left behind many sons and daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT *) It is told that when Härwed Ulf was to bring this his bride home, some fighters emerged from the forest to fiercely abduct Magnhild, and had their captains disguised as a troll, so as to be able to frighten her guardians so muc
2 CONC h more easily. But Härwed did not let themselves be horrified, but assigned the troll a cut on the neck so hard that the head flew off, after which the other pre-stretched lupo went back to the forest, and Härwed was allowed to br
2 CONC ing home his Magnhild. From that day on, Härwed changed his shield brand, which had previously been a running Ulf, and had this set a headless troll, called Trolle in st. f. Ulf, and descended from him the then infamous Trollestäg
2 CONC t.
2 CONT
